{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "demonstration",
      "description": "The ordered list of every frame label supplied in the video cycle, including the last frame. This preserves the demonstrated manipulation: which peg was selected, where along that peg it was grasped, and the approach and insertion geometry. Write the list at the video cycle and carry it unchanged through every act cycle. Before a video has been supplied the value is an empty list. e.g. [\"V0\", \"V16\", \"V32\", \"V48\", \"V64\", \"V80\", \"V96\", \"V112\", \"V128\", \"V144\", \"V160\", \"V176\", \"V192\", \"V208\", \"V222\"]",
      "value_type": "keyframe"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "demonstration",
      "description": "At the video cycle, write all supplied V frame labels in chronological order, including the last frame. At every act cycle copy the incoming list unchanged; do not replace demonstration images with execution images. If no demonstration has yet been supplied, write an empty list. e.g. [\"V0\", \"V16\", \"V32\", \"V48\", \"V64\", \"V80\", \"V96\", \"V112\", \"V128\", \"V144\", \"V160\", \"V176\", \"V192\", \"V208\", \"V222\"]"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At a video cycle output memory only. Execution starts with the open hand above the scene. Read demonstration to identify the peg actually lifted, the particular colored end between the fingers, and the box face entered by the other end. Track the selected peg backward to its initial position; do not select by color alone, since multiple pegs can share colors. Distinguish grasping an end from inserting that end: the free end enters the box. The selected box face can be on either image side. Robot left is +y, which appears on the right of the front image.\n\nUse calibrated image geometry rather than copying episode-specific world coordinates. Recover tabletop peg axes and endpoints by intersecting camera rays with their appropriate height planes. Fit the box footprint and orientation from its edges. Use the wrist image to refine visible geometry. Preserve the demonstrated grasp location along the selected colored segment, not the peg midpoint. Recover this location from the closing and lifting demonstration frames, consulting unobstructed earlier frames. Use a downward hand, roll approximately -pi and pitch approximately zero. At pickup, align the hand's longitudinal axis with the peg and close across its width; choose the yaw equivalent that matches the demonstration.\n\nApply the following rules according to the observed pose, held object, and motion frames, not according to cycle number. If the hand is empty and not yet above the demonstrated grasp location, move open to that location at z=0.20 m with the pickup yaw. If it is open above that location near z=0.20 m, descend to the same nominal x,y at z=0.01 m and close. Estimate the nominal location from the peg geometry, not from the slightly displaced reported TCP after the approach. If the fingers have closed around the selected peg near table height, lift closed to z=0.20 m at that same nominal location and yaw.\n\nIf the selected peg has been lifted but has not yet been transported to the insertion approach, move closed to the demonstrated elevated approach pose. This pose is directly above the low horizontal insertion staging pose, with TCP height about 0.19 m. Rotate about the vertical axis so that the free peg tip points normally into the demonstrated box face. Account for which end is held: matching the box normal alone does not determine the correct yaw, and the wrong pi-equivalent yaw reverses the tip. Determine the staging x,y from the demonstrated pre-insertion geometry, box location, peg length, and actual grasp offset. In particular, use the frames after transport and before the first horizontal advance to distinguish this pose from the pickup lift. Do not move directly from pickup into the opening.\n\nIf the hand is at the elevated insertion staging pose, descend closed to the same intended staging x,y, at TCP z approximately 0.039–0.041 m. Correct transport tracking error using the intended box-relative staging geometry rather than preserving the reported transport endpoint. The peg stays horizontal and its centerline must match the opening height. If the hand is at this low staging pose and the free tip is still outside the opening, advance approximately 0.05 m along the inward face normal, maintaining alignment and a TCP height approximately 0.037–0.039 m. Small lateral corrections should align the free tip with the opening, not the TCP with the opening.\n\nIf that initial horizontal advance has completed, continue inward to the demonstrated terminal insertion configuration. Infer the required endpoint from the retained demonstration's final frames and the current box and held-peg geometry. Do not use a fixed second advance: the examples include both a short remaining advance of about 0.01 m and advances exceeding 0.10 m. Peg length and the distance from the grasp to the free tip matter. Fit the visible peg and gripper geometry at the final demonstration pose to determine the desired TCP position; maintain the insertion yaw and opening height. Keep the gripper closed throughout lifting, transport, descent, and insertion. The red box indicator is evidence of success, not a cue to release the peg.\n\nUse the current motion frames to distinguish a completed lift from completed transport and a completed descent from completed horizontal insertion. Compare the current configuration to the retained demonstration when the current wrist view shows only the held peg or table; do not interpret an occluded box as a missing box. Allow approximately centimetre-scale tracking error when recognizing a completed long move, but compute the next waypoint from the intended geometry.\n\nFor an unlisted or uncertain configuration, localize the held peg, box, and closest demonstrated configuration again. If holding the peg with uncertain clearance, lift vertically to safe clearance before correcting alignment; if empty, approach the demonstrated peg open. Never insert blindly when the free tip is visibly misaligned. Use a small corrective waypoint when necessary, and never issue an identical TCP waypoint merely to change the gripper. No purposeless waiting is required."
}
